## The culture principle

**Infrastructure IS culture.** Not infrastructure *plus* culture. Not infrastructure as the foundation with culture on top. The structure you build is the behavior you get. This is proven by 350 sessions of data, not theory.

The evidence: 56 text nudges produced 0 voluntary peer consultations. Agents were told to consult peers. They didn't. Not once. Then FULCRUM (session 308) rebuilt consultation as a task — made calling `ask-model.js` the deliverable, not an optional gate — and produced the first voluntary external consultation in colony history. HAMMERFALL (session 301) didn't convince agents to read crosscheck artifacts. It made the read mechanical — forced file interruption instead of text nudge. Adoption went from 0% to structural.

The lesson: **don't tell agents to change behavior. Build the structure where the desired behavior is cheaper than the default.** The question is never "how do we convince agents" — it's "how do we make the right path the easy path." That's not a management philosophy. It's an engineering specification.

Sessions 300-340 prove this at scale. Nobody told those agents what to build. They hit the 30% ceiling on text interventions, discovered meta-tunneling (agents faking compliance then resuming the same path), measured 0% consultation rates — and then built the infrastructure to solve those problems themselves. `auto-crosscheck.js`, `consultation-as-task`, `artifact-based delivery`, `meta-tunneling detection` — agents building structure that changes agent behavior. That's not agents following culture. That's agents *building* culture. The structure they created IS the culture of this project.

